WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.150
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:03.129
You'll never guess where I am...I'm in an
airport!

00:00:03.129 --> 00:00:07.180
I was last in this airport so recently that
my log-in to the wireless hasn't expired.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.719
Ok, so, yesterday, Subbable was acquired by
Patreon; that was great news, and more great

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:16.470
news: today is the publication day of Hold
Me Closer, David Levithan's musical novel

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.320
companion to the book we wrote together -- Will
Grayson, Will Grayson.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.320
It's like the actual Tiny Cooper musical from
Will Grayson, Will Grayson; it's fantastic.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.820
And in even more great news: the Paper Towns
trailer comes out on Thursday which is why

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.599
I'm in the airport; because I have to go be
on The Today Show.

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:34.360
Also, 100% of my current children have vomited
on me in the past 24 hours, so I'm a little

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.880
nervous that I might get a stomach bug and
then throw up on The Today Show, which would

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.820
be very problematic on a number of fronts,
but especially, like, the GIFs?

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.180
I just keep imagining the GIFs of me throwing
up on Matt Lauer...

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.160
Anyway, giving the forthcoming release of
the trailer, I've been thinking a lot about

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.650
the phrase "Paper Towns".

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:53.880
Like in the book it means different things
to Q, the narrator, at different points in

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:54.880
the story.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.820
But in real life, as you know Hank, the phrase
"Paper Towns" is about copyright.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.780
Like mapmakers create fictitious entries in
their map to make sure that no one else is

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:02.780
copying them.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.180
Like if you put my fake place on your map,
I know that you've stolen from me.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.450
Every map company does this, Hank.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.080
Like Google Maps does it all the time; they
probably created a paper town called Argelton

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.080
in England.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.080
But you see it all the time.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.970
It's usually not paper towns; it's usually
like paper streets or paper bridges.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:20.660
My favorite example of this aside from Agloe,
New York (the one that I use in Paper Towns)

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.380
is Beatosu, Ohio, which is a town created
by mapmakers who were fans of the University

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.120
of Michigan's football team and wanted them
to "beat OSU."

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.390
But Hank, these copyright traps aren't just
limited to mapmaking; you see them everywhere.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:36.670
In encyclopedias, in dictionaries, in paleontology
textbooks.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.850
Like in Paper Towns, Margo's dog is named
Myrna Mountweasel in honor of this fictitious

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.920
entry from the New Columbia Encyclopedia about
Lillian Virginia Mountweasel.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.940
The encyclopedia said that she was a fountain
designer and photographer who died in an explosion

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.550
while on assignment for Combustibles Magazine.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:56.280
I'm just totally fascinated by how much fictional
information is floating around in the non-fictional

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.280
world, you know?

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.200
Like Google recently used fictitious entries
against Bing.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.590
The Billboard Charts have used them; but my
favorite example comes from the New Oxford

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.920
American Dictionary.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.010
The word esquivesence...nope, it's esquivalence...

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:10.750
I'm sorry, sometimes I mix up my nonexistent
words.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.540
Esquivalence or maybe it's esquivalience...it
occurs to me that I have no idea how to pronounce

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.620
this fictional word.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.530
Anyway, it's defined as avoiding one's official
responsibilities, you know, as a thieving

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:19.560
lexicographer would.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:25.190
And the crazy thing about esquivalience is
that like Agloe, NY and many other fictitious

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.390
entries, it has kind of become real.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:31.390
Like it's in a bunch of dictionaries now,
and the Apple dictionary even gives it a fictional

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.390
etymology.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.020
Now, like obviously this is an appealing idea
for a writer because I need to believe that

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:40.210
made-up stories can really matter in the lives
of real people.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:45.590
But it also reminds me that yes, obviously
language shapes the dictionary, but the dictionary

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:46.819
also shapes the language.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:50.970
And Hank, I guess I kind of think it is the
official responsibility of us as human beings

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:56.330
to try to imagine the world around us as complexly
and interestingly as we can.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.620
And on that front at least, let us endeavor
not to be esquivalient.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.580
I hope you like the Paper Towns movie trailer;
it comes out on Thursday.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.420
I will see you on Friday!

